,{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 210","reason":"Drawing through C a line parallel to AD produces a triangle with sides 5,12,13. Since 5²+12²=13², its area is 30 and its height to the 13-unit side is 60/13. The trapezoid area is ((52+39)/2)(60/13)=210.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"auxiliary-right-triangle","label":"Construct a 5-12-13 triangle","nodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_177","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_067","cnm_112"],"id":"solution:auxiliary-right-triangle:b746884d00fe","label":"Create a 5-12-13 triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-right-triangle":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_p149"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_p149","cnm_055","cnm_067","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 30 square units","reason":"The side lengths are √25=5, √144=12, and √169=13. Since 5²+12²=13², the triangle is right and its area is 5·12/2=30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_8_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p089"],"id":"m1","label":"Square sides and a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_075","cnm_177","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_075","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 90°","reason":"Let α = ∠BAE = ∠ACD. Since ∠AEB = 120°, ∠ABC = 60° - α, while ∠ACB = 60° + α. Hence ∠BAC = 60°. Writing AC = s and AB = 2s, the cosine law gives BC² = 3s². Therefore AC² + BC² = AB², so ∠ACB = 90°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Cosine law and Pythagorean converse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_055"],"id":"altitude-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Method 2: An altitude and side squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 90 degrees","reason":"Put alpha=angle ACD=angle BAE. The equilateral triangle gives angle ACE=60+alpha and angle AEC=60, so angle CAE=60-alpha and angle BAC=60. Writing AC=s and AB=2s, the cosine rule gives BC^2=4s^2+s^2-2(2s)(s)(1/2)=3s^2. Hence AB^2=AC^2+BC^2, so angle ACB=90 degrees.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Cosine rule and right-triangle test","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 6/5","reason":"Place the centers at (0,0), (3,0), and (0,4). The contact points are (1,0), (0,1), and (9/5,8/5). Their triangle has area |(-1)(8/5)-(1)(4/5)|/2=6/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177"],"id":"solution:subtract-corner-triangles:1c5232388eb2","label":"Subtract the three corner triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-corner-triangles":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 17/6","reason":"Since 6² + 8² = 10², ABC is right at A, and its hypotenuse median AD is 5. Its area is 24, so both subtriangles have area 12. Their semiperimeters are 8 and 9, giving radii 12/8 and 12/9, whose sum is 17/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-triangle-median","label":"Right triangle and median","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-median":["cnm_177","cnm_187","cnm_113","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_187","cnm_113","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hypotenuse-median-inradii","label":"Use the median and inradii","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hypotenuse-median-inradii":["cnm_177","cnm_187","cnm_113","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_187","cnm_113","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"24","reason":"At the diagonal intersection O, OA = 3 and OB = 4. Since 3² + 4² = AB², angle AOB is 90°. The parallelogram is a rhombus, with area 6 × 8 / 2 = 24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_190_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"id":"diagonal-intersection","label":"Establish perpendicular diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-intersection":["cnm_177","cnm_190","cnm_189"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (tangent)","reason":"5² + 12² = 13², so OA is perpendicular to AB. Since A is on the circle, line AB is tangent at A.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_261_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_064"],"id":"m1","label":"Establish perpendicularity from the lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_177","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_261","cnm_250","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 73","reason":"PA=18 and PC(PC+7)=8·18=144, giving PC=9. The cosine rule gives AC²=243, so PA²=PC²+AC² and AC is perpendicular to CD. Thus AD is a diameter, and r²=(243+49)/4=73.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-angle-diameter","label":"Identify a diameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-angle-diameter":["cnm_h091","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 127; s = √(85 + 42√2)","reason":"Set C = (0,0), A = (s,0), B = (0,s), P = (x,y), and t = s². The distances give x² + y² = 36, 2sx = t - 85, and 2sy = t - 13, hence t² - 170t + 3697 = 0. Thus t = 85 ± 42√2; interiority gives x > 0 and t > 85, so a + b = 85 + 42 = 127.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"quarter-turn-construction","label":"90° rotation and right triangles","nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_137","cnm_138"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_115","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"],"id":"solution:quarter-turn-construction:9bdb663b9601","label":"Rotate to construct a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quarter-turn-construction":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_174","cnm_245","cnm_115","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 8.64","reason":"The hypotenuse is 6, so the legs are 18/5 and 24/5. The area is (1/2)(18/