ngle at R, R lies on the circle with diameter PQ and radius 4. Each of the two lines at distance 3 from PQ intersects this circle twice, giving 2 + 2 + 4 = 8 positions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pythagorean-altitude","label":"Pythagorean equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-altitude":["cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_221"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinates-and-pythagorean-equality","label":"Use coordinates and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-pythagorean-equality":["cnm_p148","cnm_h230","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_h230","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 45°","reason":"For the acute angle θ, tan θ=|(2-1/3)/(1+2/3)|=1. Therefore θ=45°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-isosceles-right-triangle","label":"Build an isosceles right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-isosceles-right-triangle":["cnm_h167","cnm_h173","cnm_177","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h167","cnm_h173","cnm_177","cnm_137"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"150°","reason":"Rotate about B through 60° taking A to C and P to P′. Then CP′=3, PP′=4, and CP=5, so ∠CP′P=90°. Triangle BPP′ is equilateral, and the interior configuration gives ∠APB=∠CP′B=90°+60°=150°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_247_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_115"],"id":"sixty-degree-rotation","label":"Rotate to construct a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sixty-degree-rotation":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_058","cnm_245","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. A + B + 210 = C","reason":"20² + 21² = 29², so the triangle is right and its hypotenuse is a diameter. C is one semicircle; the other consists of A, B, and the triangle. The triangle's area is 20 × 21/2 = 210, hence C = A + B + 210.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_p122"],"id":"compare-semicircles","label":"Compare the two semicircles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-semicircles":["cnm_p150","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_p148","cnm_250","cnm_p122"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 240/13","reason":"Since 5^2 + 12^2 = 13^2, [ABC] = 30. Also AM = 1 and BN = 8. The corner triangles AMJ and BNK are similar to ABC with scale factors 1/13 and 8/13. Thus the pentagon area is 30(1 - 1/169 - 64/169) = 240/13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_270"],"id":"subtract-similar-corners","label":"Subtract two similar corner triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-similar-corners":["cnm_p150","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_270"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 8.64","reason":"Since 3² + 4² = 5², the triangle is right and its hypotenuse is 2 × 3 = 6. The scale factor is 6/5, so its area is (1/2)(18/5)(24/5) = 216/25 = 8.64.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p185","cnm_p188","cnm_p145"],"id":"right-triangle-diameter","label":"Use the right triangle and its diameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-diameter":["cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_p148","cnm_p185","cnm_p188","cnm_p145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 24/5","reason":"Faces ABC and ABD are 3-4-5 right triangles. Their perpendiculars to AB have the same foot H, with CH = DH = 12/5. Since CD² = CH² + DH², DH is perpendicular to CH and hence to plane ABC. The base area is 6, so V = (1/3)·6·(12/5) = 24/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"],"id":"solution:geometric-height:2046c54e9b77","label":"Find the height geometrically","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-height":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","cnm_h120"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","cnm_h120","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_h151","cnm_174"],"id":"coordinate-distance-equations","label":"Subtract spatial distance equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-distance-equations":["cnm_h111","cnm_h154","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_h154","cnm_220","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_h151","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, √7","reason":"Place A=(0,0), B=(s,0), C=(s/2,√3s/2), and let u=s². Subtracting distance equations gives P=((u−2)/(2s),(u−4)/(2√3s)). AP²=1 then yields u²−8u+7=0. The root u=1 puts P outside; u=7 gives interior barycentric coordinates (4/7,2/7,1/7). The distance differences uniquely determine P, so s=√7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"context