Together with DH perpendicular to AB, this makes DH a height above ABC. Thus V=(1/3)*6*(12/5)=24/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"],"id":"common-foot-and-perpendicular-height","label":"Find the perpendicular height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-foot-and-perpendicular-height":["cnm_h111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_h120"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_h120","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 6/23","reason":"Place the radius-1, radius-2, and radius-3 centers at (0,0), (3,0), and (0,4). For inner center (x,y), subtracting squared-distance equations gives x=1-r/3 and y=1-r/2. Substitution into x²+y²=(r+1)² yields (23r-6)(r+6)=0. The positive radius is 6/23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:center-distance-equations:6c5310eb8629","label":"Use distances between the centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-distance-equations":["cnm_h181","cnm_177","cnm_h173","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_177","cnm_h173","cnm_224","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 79","reason":"A 60-degree rotation about A creates a triangle with sides 6, 8, and 10, hence a right angle. The resulting included angle for sides 8 and 6 is 150 degrees, so the equilateral side satisfies s^2=100+48sqrt(3). Its area is sqrt(3)s^2/4=36+25sqrt(3), approximately 79.30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sixty-degree-rotation-general-cosine","label":"Rotate to a 6-8-10 triangle and use its derived angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sixty-degree-rotation-general-cosine":["cnm_247","cnm_177","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h060","cnm_h064","cnm_276"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h060","cnm_h064","cnm_276"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_075"],"id":"rotation-and-right-triangles","label":"Create a 6-8-10 right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rotation-and-right-triangles":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_140"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_140","cnm_245","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4.8","reason":"Since 6² + 8² = 10², ∠C = 90° and the altitude to AB is h = 6·8/10 = 4.8. For tangency point T, h ≤ CT ≤ 2r. Equality is attained by the circle whose diameter joins C to its perpendicular foot on AB. Finally, ∠QCR = 90°, so QR is a diameter and equals h = 4.8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_260"],"id":"altitude-diameter-bound","label":"Bound the diameter and construct the smallest circle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-diameter-bound":["cnm_065","cnm_250","cnm_261","cnm_254"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_250","cnm_261","cnm_254","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_260"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 12","reason":"The 6-8-10 triangle has area 24 and semiperimeter 12, so its inradius is 2. Moving every side inward by 1 gives a triangle homothetic about the incenter with scale (2−1)/2=1/2. Thus P travels (6+8+10)/2=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_177","cnm_p148"],"id":"incenter-scaling","label":"Find the smaller triangle traced by the center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"incenter-scaling":["cnm_260","cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_275","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_260","cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_275","cnm_272","cnm_065","cnm_177","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 625/144","reason":"On the complete grid, reflection pairs reciprocal tangents, so the product is 1. Triangles containing O=(0,0), with their right angle at O or on an axis, contribute 24/625 altogether. For interior right-angle vertices, the only nonunit aggregate factors occur at (2,1), (3,3), (4,2), and (4,4): respectively 1/2, 3/2, 2, and 4. Their product is 6. Removing all O-triangles therefore leaves 1/((24/625)·6)=625/144.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_131","cnm_016"],"id":"reflection-and-boundary-correction","label":"Complete the grid and use reciprocal pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reflection-and-boundary-correction":["cnm_276","cnm_132","cnm_174","cnm_177","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_132","cnm_174","cnm_177","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_082","cnm_131","cnm_016"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 15/8 inches","reason":"Let M be the midpoint of AB and X the crease's intersection with AC. Then AM = 5/2 and MX is perpendicular to AB. Triangles AMX and ACB are similar, with AM corresponding to AC, so MX = 3(5/2)/4 = 15/8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_131","cnm_064","cnm_055","cnm_177","cnm_270"],"id":"solution:similar-right-triangles:0a3dbc7f20c5","label":"Method 1: Similar right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-right-triangles":["cnm_132","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_132","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_131","cnm_064","cnm_055","cnm_177","cnm_270"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_131","cnm_064","cnm_055","cnm_177","cnm_075"],"id":"equidistance-pythagoras","label":"Method 2: Equal distances and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equidistance-pythagoras":["cnm_132","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_132","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_042","cnm_131","cnm_064","cnm_055","cnm_177","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, none of the listed relations is necessarily true","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(0,6), P=(1,2), with D=AP∩BC=(2,4), E=BP∩AC=(0,12/5), F=CP∩AB=(3/2,0). Opposite triangles AFP and CDP have areas 3/2 and 3, and unequal longest-to-shortest side ratios 2sqrt(5)/3 and sqrt(17/5). Also AFPE has a right angle while BDPF has none, so the quadrilaterals are not all similar.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_177","cnm_268","cnm_110"],"id":"construct-counterexample","label":"Test all four claims with a counterexample","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-counterexample":["cnm_071","cnm_270","cnm_120","cnm_p148","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_071","cnm_270","cnm_120","cnm_p148","cnm_h173","cnm_082","cnm_177","cnm_268","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 81","reason":"Write the sides as a,a+d,a+2d with positive integers a,d. The Pythagorean equation reduces to (a-3d)(a+d)=0, so the sides are 3d,4d,5d. Only 81 among the choices is divisible by 3,4, or 5; it occurs as 3·27.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_conc